    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(77, 12), (49, 34), (22, 14), (79, 73), (68, 16)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 25.0, 47.0, 40.0]\n    generator_capacities = [145.0, 172.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 500.0  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 220.0  # kV\n    line_capacity = 100.0  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 100000.0  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 50.0  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 Minimum-cost transmission design with square-law loss costs for a five-node system\n\nOverview\nOur client is asking us to lay out a cost-effective transmission network that links two generator sites to three consumer sites. The sites are spread across a map, and we are free to propose lines between any pair of nodes; there are no pre-defined corridors or mandatory routes. Once a line is built, it can move power in either direction and must respect a rated throughput limit. The challenge is to balance the upfront cost of building longer lines against the ongoing cost of resistive heating losses, which rise rapidly as flows increase.\n\nProvided inputs\n- Node locations (map coordinates, km): Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (77, 12)\n  - N1 (generator): (49, 34)\n  - N2 (consumer): (22, 14)\n  - N3 (consumer): (79, 73)\n  - N4 (consumer): (68, 16)\n- Node roles (generator vs. consumer at each site): ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n- Consumer power requirements (MW): Consumer demands: N2: 25.0 MW, N3: 47.0 MW, N4: 40.0 MW\n- Generator maximum outputs (MW): Generator capacities: N0: 145.0 MW, N1: 172.0 MW\n- Copper conductor resistivity: 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper)\n- Conductor cross-sectional area: 500.0 mm\u00b2\n- System operating voltage: 220.0 kV\n- Rated transfer limit per built line (MW, either direction): 100.0 MW\n- Line construction cost per kilometer: $100,000/km\n- Monetary cost per unit of energy lost to heating: $50.0/MWh\n\nPlanning context and rules\n- Build decisions: We can choose to build a line between any pair of nodes. Power can flow only across the lines we decide to build.\n- Directionality and limits: Each built line is bidirectional and subject to the same rated capacity in both directions.\n- Service obligation: Every consumer must be served at least their required demand; surplus at any node is acceptable. Each generator\u2019s output cannot exceed its specified maximum.\n- Nodal accounting: For planning, we treat power flow as conserved at each node. In other words, we do not subtract physical line losses from nodal balances; instead, we account for losses as a separate financial penalty.\n\nCost drivers we must capture\n- Construction cost: Building a line costs money proportional to the distance between its endpoints at the rate provided. Longer spans reduce the number of alternative routes but raise upfront spending.\n- Resistive heating cost: Transmitting power through copper conductors produces resistive losses that follow a square law with current (the familiar I-squared-R effect). In practical terms, pushing twice as much power through the same line quadruples the loss. For planning, we translate that physical loss into dollars by applying the given monetary rate to the energy lost to heating.\n- Physics link to design choices:\n  - Resistance increases with length and decreases with conductor cross-sectional area; the copper material property is provided.\n  - For a fixed operating voltage, current is proportional to power flow, so the loss scales with the square of power divided by voltage. Higher voltage dramatically cuts current and therefore heating, introducing an effective 1 over voltage squared benefit in the loss term.\n  - The cost of losses is driven by the relationship: loss is proportional to resistance times the square of power flow divided by the square of operating voltage; we then monetize that loss using the specified rate. We must not rely on linear approximations or proxies here\u2014getting the square-law behavior right is critical to avoid underestimating heating costs on heavily loaded paths.\n\nWhat the solution must decide\n- Which lines to build between the given node locations.\n- How much power to route across each built line (in either direction), without exceeding the line\u2019s rated capacity.\n- How to dispatch the available generation to meet all consumer needs.\n\nObjective\nMinimize the combined cost of:\n- Line construction (distance-based), and\n- Resistive heating costs, which we compute using the square-law loss model tied to resistance and operating voltage and monetize at the given rate.\n\nWhy this matters\nUnder a simple \u201csum of flows\u201d approach, operations on a few heavily loaded lines might look acceptable on paper but will incur disproportionately large heating costs in reality. By explicitly modeling the square-law relationship and the role of voltage and conductor properties, we\u2019ll select a layout that avoids expensive bottlenecks, leverages higher-voltage operation to suppress current, and balances slightly longer routes against lower losses where it pays off.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 Minimum-cost transmission design with square-law loss costs for a five-node system\n\nOverview\nOur client is asking us to lay out a cost-effective transmission network that links two generator sites to three consumer sites. The sites are spread across a map, and we are free to propose lines between any pair of nodes; there are no pre-defined corridors or mandatory routes. Once a line is built, it can move power in either direction and must respect a rated throughput limit. The challenge is to balance the upfront cost of building longer lines against the ongoing cost of resistive heating losses, which rise rapidly as flows increase.\n\nProvided inputs\n- Node locations (map coordinates, km): {NODE_COORDINATES}\n- Node roles (generator vs. consumer at each site): {NODE_TYPES}\n- Consumer power requirements (MW): {POWER_DEMANDS}\n- Generator maximum outputs (MW): {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}\n- Copper conductor resistivity: {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}\n- Conductor cross-sectional area: {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}\n- System operating voltage: {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}\n- Rated transfer limit per built line (MW, either direction): {LINE_CAPACITY}\n- Line construction cost per kilometer: {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}\n- Monetary cost per unit of energy lost to heating: {LOSS_COST_RATE}\n\nPlanning context and rules\n- Build decisions: We can choose to build a line between any pair of nodes. Power can flow only across the lines we decide to build.\n- Directionality and limits: Each built line is bidirectional and subject to the same rated capacity in both directions.\n- Service obligation: Every consumer must be served at least their required demand; surplus at any node is acceptable. Each generator\u2019s output cannot exceed its specified maximum.\n- Nodal accounting: For planning, we treat power flow as conserved at each node. In other words, we do not subtract physical line losses from nodal balances; instead, we account for losses as a separate financial penalty.\n\nCost drivers we must capture\n- Construction cost: Building a line costs money proportional to the distance between its endpoints at the rate provided. Longer spans reduce the number of alternative routes but raise upfront spending.\n- Resistive heating cost: Transmitting power through copper conductors produces resistive losses that follow a square law with current (the familiar I-squared-R effect). In practical terms, pushing twice as much power through the same line quadruples the loss. For planning, we translate that physical loss into dollars by applying the given monetary rate to the energy lost to heating.\n- Physics link to design choices:\n  - Resistance increases with length and decreases with conductor cross-sectional area; the copper material property is provided.\n  - For a fixed operating voltage, current is proportional to power flow, so the loss scales with the square of power divided by voltage. Higher voltage dramatically cuts current and therefore heating, introducing an effective 1 over voltage squared benefit in the loss term.\n  - The cost of losses is driven by the relationship: loss is proportional to resistance times the square of power flow divided by the square of operating voltage; we then monetize that loss using the specified rate. We must not rely on linear approximations or proxies here\u2014getting the square-law behavior right is critical to avoid underestimating heating costs on heavily loaded paths.\n\nWhat the solution must decide\n- Which lines to build between the given node locations.\n- How much power to route across each built line (in either direction), without exceeding the line\u2019s rated capacity.\n- How to dispatch the available generation to meet all consumer needs.\n\nObjective\nMinimize the combined cost of:\n- Line construction (distance-based), and\n- Resistive heating costs, which we compute using the square-law loss model tied to resistance and operating voltage and monetize at the given rate.\n\nWhy this matters\nUnder a simple \u201csum of flows\u201d approach, operations on a few heavily loaded lines might look acceptable on paper but will incur disproportionately large heating costs in reality. By explicitly modeling the square-law relationship and the role of voltage and conductor properties, we\u2019ll select a layout that avoids expensive bottlenecks, leverages higher-voltage operation to suppress current, and balances slightly longer routes against lower losses where it pays off."
